# Seed dictionary is the single source of reference content + e2e fixtures

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 core rule.

## Use This When

- Keep this rule active for any work that adds or changes a USER-FACING knowledge
  capability (a node kind, edge type, projection/app, access dimension, lifecycle
  action, lens), or any e2e spec that needs seed data.

## Stop Here If

- Stop after the coverage + dedup obligations are clear; this is a leaf rule.

## Descend To

- Methodology + execution: the **`seed-curator`** agent (`.claude/agents/seed-curator.md`).
- The dictionary itself: `seed/` (`@workspace/seed`) and its `README.md`.

## The single source

`@workspace/seed` (the `seed/` workspace) is the ONE home of reference content. It serves
four masters at once: e2e consistency, demo population, no-more-hand-built-trees, and
self-documentation. The `seed-curator` agent owns it. All content is created at RUNTIME
through the live `/author/graph/*` endpoints under RLS — NEVER via migrations (that
poisons the author identity-sync worker).

`bun run seed --manifest=<file>` dumps the seeded `{scenario: {ref: id}}` map as JSON —
the machine-readable dictionary (gitignored runtime artifact) for grounding/verification
and LLM↔human feedback. Generate it to confirm a scenario materialized; read it on hand-off
to know what already exists. The `seed-curator` agent both generates and consumes it.

## Required behavior

1. **Feature work extends the seed.** When a change adds or alters a user-facing capability,
   the seed dictionary MUST gain a scenario (or an extended one) that demonstrates it —
   declarative data + a real Lexical body + a one-line `summary`, wired into a preset. Do
   this as the **close-out of the feature**, by **chaining the `seed-curator` agent** when
   you can spawn sub-agents, or INLINE following its methodology when you cannot. A purely
   internal change (no node/edge/view/lifecycle a user can see) is exempt — say so.

2. **e2e draws seed data from the catalog.** An e2e spec that needs a tree, actors, or a
   projection takes them from `@workspace/seed` — `materializeFixture(scenario, tenant)` +
   `seedClientFor(actor)` (the shared `/author/graph/*` create-vocabulary), and asserts
   against the scenario's named `ref`s. Relevant fixtures live in `seed/src/catalog/*`, NOT
   inline in the spec.

3. **No duplicated test data.** Do NOT re-implement `createFolder` / `createDoc` / inline
   resource trees in a spec when the shared client + a catalog fixture exist. Reducing
   duplication and keeping one create-vocabulary is the whole point.

## Rationale

One dictionary means the demo, the tests, and the docs tell the exact same story, and
LLM↔human feedback can name a resource by its `ref` rather than a UUID. Drift (a feature
with no seed; a spec with its own inline tree) is the thing this rule prevents.
